/4-T-001-attempt.xml</File><File digest="973a6df767f0d7af728dbd42ac5a8ba9c96bfde92767b10bdc8af1e3fa6db5b1">.ngrace/changes/active/C-BUNDLE-BASE-REF/run/5-T-001-attempt.xml</File><File digest="227bdbb8603a3e87a3e1f291680e5c3fd8c17d73a2f4b1e1b56e94bef0ed220e">.ngrace/changes/active/C-BUNDLE-BASE-REF/run/6-T-002-progress.xml</File><File digest="f5a768b01ed1df1f99b52b990e23940749fb8923bf3e0369ac34d2c455e0cef1">.ngrace/changes/active/C-BUNDLE-BASE-REF/run/7-T-002-attempt.xml</File><File digest="4f3cfbb573825e0d09593a3bb39d252651a3dd113b9f115c11ada4dd30fab598">.ngrace/changes/active/C-BUNDLE-BASE-REF/run/8-T-002-attempt.xml</File><File digest="6d4d491b5b41777f087349dc329b7213ecbb713154867f610d5278904398acd2">.ngrace/changes/active/C-BUNDLE-BASE-REF/run/9-T-002-attempt.xml</File><File digest="de4fd092cad88f8b9afa83a28be529f819487de8abad30bebb2db59c7c2932ac">src/gates/command.ts</File><File digest="1286333eb4d7030e8efc1b8093b2e6c068ecc58624d46b24d61d82a9f13b560c">src/gates/core.test.ts</File><File digest="dab12db279e0b9658b3ddc89c3c9497485b4641eec733db2689ad7c1dbd05ee8">src/gates/ledger.ts</File><File digest="73210d82baabd95cdfbb2776fab396a55758478a920ec67ff07250e77b7b2809">src/review/command.ts</File><File digest="97fd2dffd60654c2fafb1b59ef40dd03fa17c7d4e3ec6179204819e2c3e20964">src/review/core.test.ts</File><File digest="3badc3c4cef76fd76f598addabec3e35d172069f13ab2c3cca88e9a285319f87">src/review/core.ts</File></WriteEvidence></Event><Event id="16" task="T-004" kind="terminal" /></Epoch-1><Verdicts><Verdict outcome="pass" scope="bundle" classification="implementation">Reviewed against the approved spec by the authority. Review 0 findings; WriteEvidence scope audit 21 paths 0 findings; attempt-pair audit 3 pairs 0 findings; scope audit not-run on a clean tree, the honest F27 case. Verified independently: suite 1464 pass 3 skip 0 fail, validate:ci exit 0.

DISCLOSURE 1, THE ROADMAP STEP COULD NOT BE BUILT AS WRITTEN. Recorded as F81, sixth such claim in this phase. It prescribed a BaseCommit child, which raises ledger.invalid-decision and would have invalidated the very approve event the record exists to key for P3.7 - extra attributes are accepted, so the attribute is the only shape that survives. It claimed a no-git porcelain fallback that already does not exist, since review becomes unable-to-determine when git fails. It named base..working-tree, which is not git syntax, while preserving a helper that is three-dot to HEAD and would have gone blind on uncommitted work. And pre-existing dirt never enters overclaims a SHA, because files dirty at approve still differ from it. The step also never said what re-approval does: five archived bundles already carry two permitting approve Decisions and permit lookup is newest-governs, so the naive reading makes a recorded fact silently rewritable. Hence first-observation-wins with a first-in-document-order reader. All four corrections were amended into the roadmap before approval.

DISCLOSURE 2, THE AUTHORITY MISCOUNTED THE REDS. The plan brief said seven behavioural criteria including AC-OVERRIDES; the approved spec names six and exempts AC-OVERRIDES under F45 as already true at HEAD, where reddening it is that criterion's own failure condition. The executor followed the spec.

DISCLOSURE 3, A DEFECT THIS BUNDLE INTRODUCED WAS REPAIRED BEFORE CLOSE, BY A SUBAGENT. listFilesChangedAgainstRecordedBase emitted one reason for three causes, so a git outage after a successful verify was reported as a dead sha - the F40 / F55 class. Now: the sha genuinely failing to verify keeps does not resolve; a failed diff says the sha resolved but the diff failed; a failed untracked walk says it diffed but status failed; each carries git's own stderr detail. Proved by discrimination that drives all three causes for real - a bogus status config, a deleted tree object, and a dead sha - then asserts three distinct masked messages; collapsed, the test fails; fixed, it passes. No existing test was weakened. The authority delegated rather than writing it, per the standing rule.

DISCLOSURE 4, F82 INDICTS THE SHARED PLAN TEMPLATE, NOT THIS BUNDLE. AC-SUITE-AND-LINT defines its lint half as 0/0 after apply and archive and is Satisfies-linked to the final task - a task that ends before the close and whose verification commands do not include lint. The recorded pass evidences the three commands it ran and nothing about the bar it is linked to. Every bundle in this phase carries that shape; the claim comes true, but the evidence lives in the verdict and the archive commit rather than in the attempt Satisfies points at, and nothing cross-checks the two. Owed to the next bundle authoring from the model plan, the route F50 and F51 took.

DISCLOSURE 5, AN AUTHORITY PROCESS ERROR CAUGHT AND CORRECTED. The F82 ledger commit was made with git add -A and swept the executor's entire implementation into a commit labelled docs-only. Caught before push, split into a decisions.md commit and the implementation commit. The lesson is the mechanical one: a ledger commit during an open window must stage its own file by name, never -A.

DISCLOSURE 6, EVIDENCE LIMITS. The recorded reds are six, one property each, per F80: the write, first-wins across a real second approve with HEAD moved between them, the recorded-base universe, the dead-sha absence, the text sentence and the JSON reason. The caveat reds deliberately use only the porcelain-ran condition, the one where text and JSON can fail alone; for not-run and git-unavailable the scope line already prints reason, so a single write would have fed both and the second red would have evidenced nothing. Untracked-enters could not be a red because porcelain already includes untracked files; it is pinned pass-side so a three-dot reuse cannot pass. F73 stands: path lists are POSIX-normalized at the helper, finding.file is still normalizeRel, and this host cannot observe a Windows separator - CI is the only observer.

DISCLOSURE 7. decisions.md was written during the open window - F81 before execution, F82 mid-window - each in its own commit ahead of the implementation commit. It is outside every bundle's ObservedWriteScope by design, and the scope audit reads working-tree porcelain, so committing removed those edits from the audit's view rather than clearing them.</Verdict></Verdicts><Decisions><Decision gate="apply" decision="permit"><Requirement id="plan-present" required="true" present="true" blocking="false">status=approved</Requirement><Requirement id="no-unresolved-satisfied-ac-clarification" required="true" present="true" blocking="false" /><Requirement id="review-verdict" required="true" present="true" blocking="false">outcome=pass</Requirement></Decision><Decision gate="archive" decision="permit"><Requirement id="no-open-epoch" required="true" present="true" blocking="false">run/ empty</Requirement></Decision></Decisions></C-BUNDLE-BASE-REF></NgraceRunLedger>
